WEBVTT

00:00:00.155 --> 00:00:02.352
This is someone shopping
with only a keyboard.

00:00:03.343 --> 00:00:05.393
They press Tab to move through the page…

00:00:05.393 --> 00:00:07.242
and the Add to cart button gets skipped.

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:08.599
They can't buy anything.

00:00:08.843 --> 00:00:09.618
That's not rare.

00:00:09.618 --> 00:00:15.268
In 2026, 95.9 percent
of the top million home pages

00:00:15.268 --> 00:00:16.667
had accessibility failures.

00:00:16.815 --> 00:00:19.814
And almost all of them come
from the same six mistakes.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.283
So we built Curb Cut.

00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:23.877
It fixes those barriers from the outside,

00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:26.521
right in your browser,
and proves every fix works.

00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:27.844
Step one.

00:00:27.844 --> 00:00:32.669
Kane, an AI testing agent, tries the task
the way a disabled person would.

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:33.320
It fails.

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:35.326
Kane tries again with a mouse.

00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:36.494
The same task works.

00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:38.809
So the website is broken, not the task.

00:00:39.085 --> 00:00:39.810
Step two.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.835
An agent writes a small, safe fix.

00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:43.240
Just rules, no code.

00:00:43.519 --> 00:00:44.444
Step three.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:47.469
Kane runs the task again with the fix,
and it passes.

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:48.762
The recording is the proof.

00:00:49.052 --> 00:00:49.927
Step four.

00:00:49.927 --> 00:00:53.509
The extension shares the proven fix
with everyone who visits that site.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.935
Some barriers are whole journeys.

00:00:55.935 --> 00:01:00.842
On mass.edu, a keyboard user reaches About,
and the menu never opens.

00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:03.862
With Curb Cut, the same keys open the menu,

00:01:03.862 --> 00:01:05.370
and Enter takes you to the page.

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.427
A screen reader just says, button.

00:01:07.564 --> 00:01:11.189
On Munich Airport, Curb Cut names it:
previous slide.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:13.361
Kane found it with the fix,
and couldn't without it.

00:01:13.609 --> 00:01:16.609
The most common failure of all is faint text.

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:19.659
Curb Cut nudges the colour
just enough to be readable,

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:20.280
and measures it.

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:24.014
And on pages we've never fixed,
there's an experiment.

00:01:24.014 --> 00:01:26.964
Jev, a fast decision model from TypeSafe,

00:01:26.964 --> 00:01:29.683
names unlabelled buttons live,
in about a second.

00:01:29.805 --> 00:01:33.130
On real sites,
13 of its 15 names were right.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.942
When it isn't sure, it stays quiet.

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.080
On OpenSeaMap, a site we've never fixed,
the keyboard skips View.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.263
One shortcut reports the barrier.

00:01:41.566 --> 00:01:43.761
Grok writes the fix
in sixteen seconds.

00:01:44.066 --> 00:01:46.925
Kane tries it. Without the fix, the
keyboard fails.

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:48.200
The mouse works.

00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:51.240
With the fix, the keyboard passes.

00:01:51.566 --> 00:01:54.968
Minutes later, after a recheck,
the fix ships in the extension.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.180
Now the same eight Tabs open View.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.879
Sea Marks is right there.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.900
Today, 106 fixes are live
in the extension.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.213
62 of them proven by Kane.

00:02:05.434 --> 00:02:07.884
And every fix can go back to the site owner:

00:02:07.884 --> 00:02:11.334
the exact change,
with Kane's recording as proof.

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:14.172
When the website fixes itself,
our patch retires.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.255
Curb Cut.

00:02:15.255 --> 00:02:17.555
Fixing the web for the people it shuts out.

00:02:17.555 --> 00:02:18.756
Verified by Kane.
